  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nLatest surface analysis shows the front along the western\nportion of the state. The surface low is just to the north of\nthe state and it has started to lift out into Canada. It will\ncontinue to move out through the afternoon. Linger rain showers\nwill continue as it gets wrap around the low, especially along \nthe international border. The area will clear out of the rain  \nshowers and low cloud cover as the front passes by. Winds will \nbe predominately out of the west by the afternoon, thanks to the\nfrontal passage, and it will bring very strong gusts across \nmuch of the state. 12Z HREF has large swath of 60-80% \nprobabilities of 50 mph or higher behind the front, starting \nfrom the west and moving east throughout the day. There is a \nnarrow swath of 20-50% probabilities of 55 mph, but it mainly \nconfined to areas west of Highway 82. As result, a High Warning\nis in effect for the western portion of the state and a Wind \nAdvisory for everywhere else through the tonight. Winds will \nslacken below warning and advisory by the late night. \n\nOn top of the strong gusty winds, dry air has started to  \nfilter into the southwestern portion of the state. It is \nevident on the water vapor imagery and the latest 12Z model \nruns continue to show this dry slot lasting through the \nafternoon. Strong winds and dry air will bring near critical \nfire weather conditions late this afternoon for the far \nsouthwestern counties. \n\nGoing forward with the rest of week, a ridge will build back\nover the region, leading a calmer day tomorrow. Winds will still\nbe elevated through the day, but lower than today. Another\nweather feature will bring another round of rain shower chances\non Wednesday. Ensemble models show the pattern will be unsettled\nfor the last half of the week and into the weekend. Another low\npressure center is poised to develop and move across the western\nstates. There is uncertainty on the progression of this feature\nat the present moment. For now, chances for rain showers will\nreturn through the weekend.",
